// spec: under --indentation-warnings, a statement indented as if it were in the body of the if, else, for or while above it, a single statement without braces it is not part of, is a warning, as clang's -Wmisleading-indentation: also through an else if and a body that is itself an unbraced if; not a body on the if's own line, a statement unindented, nor one after braces (clang warns at the same five lines)
// found by kovarex (2026-11-10): "It is good to have warning for this, as it looks like the person writing this made mistake"; under a flag while the converted game has 4 such places, each the converter's re-indenting of an #ifdef block
// kpp: --indentation-warnings
// warning: misleading_indentation_warns.kpp:16: warning: this statement is indented as if it were in the body of the `if` above it, which is only the statement on line 15: put both in braces, or unindent this one
// warning: misleading_indentation_warns.kpp:20: warning: this statement is indented as if it were in the body of the `for` above it, which is only the statement on line 19: put both in braces, or unindent this one
// warning: misleading_indentation_warns.kpp:28: warning: this statement is indented as if it were in the body of the `else` above it, which is only the statement on line 27: put both in braces, or unindent this one
// warning: misleading_indentation_warns.kpp:37: warning: this statement is indented as if it were in the body of the `if` above it, which is only the statement on line 36: put both in braces, or unindent this one
// warning: misleading_indentation_warns.kpp:42: warning: this statement is indented as if it were in the body of the `if` above it, which is only the statement on line 41: put both in braces, or unindent this one
int doSomething() { return 1; }
int doSomethingElse() { return 2; }
int main()
{
  int a = 1, b = 2, r = 0;
  if (a == b)
    r += doSomething();
    r += doSomethingElse();
  if (a == b) r += 1; r += 2;
  for (int i = 0; i < 2; ++i)
    r += i;
    r += 10;
  while (r < 0)
    r++;
  r += 1;
  if (a)
    r = 1;
  else
    r = 2;
    r = 3;
  if (a)
  {
    r = 1;
  }
    r = 4;
  if (a == 1)
    if (b == 2)
      r = 5;
      r = 6;
  if (a == 1)
    r = 7;
  else if (b == 2)
    r = 8;
    r = 9;
  std::println(r);
  return 0;
}
